          <div class="space-y-8">
            <div class="text-left">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-neutral-900">
                Can a door that won’t lock always be repaired?
              </h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-lg text-neutral-700">
                Often, yes — but the repair depends on the cause. If the door has dropped, alignment and hinge work may restore locking without replacing the mechanism. If the cylinder or mechanism has failed internally, replacement of that component is usually required.
              </p>
            </div>
            
            <div class="text-left">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-neutral-900">
                Do you repair doors that won’t close properly (catching or scraping)?
              </h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-lg text-neutral-700">
                Yes — catching, scraping, and stiff closing are commonly caused by hinge wear, door drop, or frame movement. Adjustment and hinge repairs typically restore smooth closing and correct latch engagement.
              </p>
            </div>
            
            <div class="text-left">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-neutral-900">
                What if the multipoint lock is the problem?
              </h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-lg text-neutral-700">
                Multipoint systems can fail, but they can also appear to fail when the door is misaligned. Accurate diagnosis avoids unnecessary parts. If a mechanism is genuinely failed, the correct replacement depends on the exact type and variant.
              </p>
            </div>
            
            <div class="text-left">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-neutral-900">
                What happens if parts aren’t available on the first visit?
              </h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-lg text-neutral-700">
                If a specialist part is needed, the door can often be stabilised and secured so it locks safely in the interim. The final repair is then completed once the correct component is sourced.
              </p>
            </div>
            
            <div class="text-left">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-neutral-900">
                Is it a landlord or tenant responsibility?
              </h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-lg text-neutral-700">
                It depends on the cause and the tenancy agreement. General wear-and-tear hardware issues and essential security faults are commonly handled as landlord repairs, while tenant-caused damage may differ. Clear notes and photos can help support whichever route applies.
              </p>
            </div>
            
            <div class="text-left">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-neutral-900">
                Can I DIY a door repair?
              </h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-lg text-neutral-700">
                Minor maintenance (like keeping seals clean and avoiding harsh force on handles) is fine. However, DIY on multipoint locks, cylinders, and alignment can easily worsen the issue or compromise security. If the door won’t lock reliably, it’s usually best treated as a security repair. Our advice: attempt only simple maintenance yourself, and seek professional help for complex or security-related door repair issues.
              </p>
            </div>
            
            <div class="text-left">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-neutral-900">
                Do you handle fire door repairs?
              </h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-lg text-neutral-700">
                Fire doors are specialised because the door’s integrity and correct closing matter. If fire doors are part of the requirement (communal or commercial), repairs should focus on maintaining correct function and fit rather than casual adjustments. All fire door repairs are carried out by members of reputable trade organisations, ensuring compliance with industry standards and regulations.
              </p>
            </div>
            
            <div class="text-left">
              <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-neutral-900">
                How can I prevent the problem coming back?
              </h3>
              <p class="mt-2 text-lg text-neutral-700">
                Avoid forcing handles, don’t lift the door by the handle to “make it lock”, and address early signs (stiff locking, uneven gaps, catching) before the mechanism wears out. Our advice is to perform periodic checks of hinges, fixings, and seals to reduce repeat failures and keep your door in good working condition.
              </p>
            </div>
